1
00:00:16,128 --> 00:00:17,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is first jhāna wholesome?

2
00:00:19,931 --> 00:00:23,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is jhāna wholesome?

3
00:00:24,074 --> 00:00:34,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's looted from sensuality and unwholesome things.

4
00:00:36,082 --> 00:00:36,523
[SPEAKER_01]: How?

5
00:00:38,744 --> 00:00:39,525
[SPEAKER_01]: By

6
00:00:45,627 --> 00:00:49,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Establishing the mind.

7
00:00:49,951 --> 00:00:51,392
[SPEAKER_01]: This is how I understand it anyway.

8
00:00:51,412 --> 00:00:56,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Establishing the mind to the extent where you sort of... beyond those things.

9
00:00:58,958 --> 00:01:00,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Theoretically, that's fine.

10
00:01:01,279 --> 00:01:04,242
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is then?

11
00:01:07,644 --> 00:01:09,666
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the principle of sanctuality?

12
00:01:13,269 --> 00:01:14,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

13
00:01:14,190 --> 00:01:27,554
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to say, seeing the seeing the seeing the pain is pleasurable?

14
00:01:27,854 --> 00:01:29,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

15
00:01:29,695 --> 00:01:30,635
[SPEAKER_00]: And how is that possible?

16
00:01:31,376 --> 00:01:35,237
[SPEAKER_00]: What assumption is required for you to keep seeing pain as

17
00:01:41,763 --> 00:01:41,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Enough.

18
00:01:43,104 --> 00:01:43,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Get back.

19
00:01:45,905 --> 00:01:53,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Something to do with...

20
00:01:53,948 --> 00:01:54,488
Assumption.

21
00:01:54,508 --> 00:01:56,529
What's the principle of assumption, of upādāna?

22
00:02:16,507 --> 00:02:22,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Assuming sort of something outside of this.

23
00:02:22,793 --> 00:02:26,757
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as we said before, you assume that the pleasure is outside of your desire.

24
00:02:27,697 --> 00:02:28,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, in the things.

25
00:02:29,079 --> 00:02:29,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

26
00:02:29,799 --> 00:02:30,280
[SPEAKER_00]: In whatever.

27
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:37,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't specify where it is, you assume it's outside of this presently enduring unpleasant desire.

28
00:02:37,851 --> 00:02:44,132
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it starts to... Well, that's why you induce a separate identity to that desire, desire... the identity of pleasure.

29
00:02:45,172 --> 00:02:47,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence the assumption of sensuality.

30
00:02:48,993 --> 00:02:59,735
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to see that it doesn't matter the extent of your pleasure, it's within the painful desire, thus it's unpleasant, you will stop regarding it as separately pleasurable.

31
00:03:03,317 --> 00:03:10,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can only see it as pleasant, see this pleasure, by seeing it separate from the pain.

32
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:11,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Outside of the pain.

33
00:03:11,441 --> 00:03:13,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's fully enclosed within the pain.

34
00:03:13,182 --> 00:03:13,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

35
00:03:14,322 --> 00:03:15,903
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to see that, you wouldn't see it as pleasant.

36
00:03:15,923 --> 00:03:19,044
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is the principle of upādāna.

37
00:03:19,064 --> 00:03:20,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

38
00:03:20,085 --> 00:03:21,465
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the principle of sensuality.

39
00:03:21,565 --> 00:03:21,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

40
00:03:22,346 --> 00:03:25,047
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is the principle that you abandon in the first jhāna.

41
00:03:26,208 --> 00:03:29,009
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, theoretically, it is still possible

42
00:03:29,962 --> 00:03:36,295
[SPEAKER_00]: to maintain some ignorance, despite developing the mind in the first jhāna, but it's highly unlikely.

43
00:03:37,237 --> 00:03:39,201
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why jhānas in themselves are very wholesome.

44
00:03:42,970 --> 00:03:48,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's unlikely you would be abandoning the wrong assumptions and principles without a degree of understanding.

45
00:03:49,574 --> 00:03:54,376
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what's called right contemplation, basically, right mind establishment.

46
00:03:54,616 --> 00:03:56,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's temporary liberation, or... Yeah.

47
00:03:56,777 --> 00:03:57,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's a liberation.

48
00:03:58,158 --> 00:04:00,319
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely undoing the assumptions.

49
00:04:00,399 --> 00:04:07,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, obviously, it's the fundamental wisdom in regard to the nature of the assumptions that will then keep you perpetually free from those.

50
00:04:08,018 --> 00:04:14,161
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you haven't developed that wisdom, then if you're not establishing the jhāna, then there's certain assumptions might take place again.

51
00:04:15,081 --> 00:04:28,688
[SPEAKER_00]: But even... even so, you could see how... how much less instruction a person who has developed mind to the extent of the first jhāna, how much less instruction that person would need, in order to then fully understand the nature of upādāna.

52
00:04:30,169 --> 00:04:31,089
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you think?

53
00:04:32,270 --> 00:04:32,810
[SPEAKER_00]: What is that?

54
00:04:34,511 --> 00:04:36,132
[SPEAKER_00]: There is not necessarily unwholesome,

55
00:04:36,973 --> 00:04:46,497
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's on the level of assuming it outside, that gets abandoned, and thus establish the first jhāna by abandoning it.

56
00:04:49,398 --> 00:04:55,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, you're withdrawn from centrality, but more specifically...

57
00:04:55,740 --> 00:04:56,761
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not just that...

58
00:04:56,781 --> 00:04:59,602
[SPEAKER_00]: The initial, the initial establishment of mind, the jhāna.

59
00:04:59,762 --> 00:05:00,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

60
00:05:00,342 --> 00:05:02,403
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking it was about...

61
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:06,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Putting yourself beyond sensuality, if you're saying something else.

62
00:05:06,051 --> 00:05:07,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's all a result of that.

63
00:05:07,832 --> 00:05:08,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

64
00:05:09,213 --> 00:05:13,495
[SPEAKER_00]: But, on a most practical level, well, what did the Buddha say?

65
00:05:14,755 --> 00:05:19,058
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the thing that ceases in the first jhāna?

66
00:05:19,378 --> 00:05:20,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

67
00:05:25,361 --> 00:05:25,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Speech.

68
00:05:25,921 --> 00:05:26,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Speech.

69
00:05:26,521 --> 00:05:26,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

70
00:05:28,102 --> 00:05:29,783
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is the necessary basis for speech?

71
00:05:33,041 --> 00:05:33,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, body.

72
00:05:34,282 --> 00:05:34,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

73
00:05:34,742 --> 00:05:37,684
[SPEAKER_01]: According to first jhāna, Tiruvannāmalai is correct.

74
00:05:37,925 --> 00:05:39,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, Pitaka Vichara.

75
00:05:39,406 --> 00:05:39,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

76
00:05:40,126 --> 00:05:41,307
[SPEAKER_01]: So, thinking and pondering.

77
00:05:41,327 --> 00:05:44,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking and exploring, thinking and pondering is a necessary basis.

78
00:05:44,730 --> 00:05:51,454
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is a necessary basis for engagement with external central objects?

79
00:05:56,758 --> 00:05:58,419
[SPEAKER_01]: For the sake of chasing pleasure.

80
00:06:00,261 --> 00:06:01,642
[SPEAKER_00]: What is a necessary basis for that?

81
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:17,602
[SPEAKER_00]: What does it need to be there enduring in order for you to be making effort and performing actions towards physical sense objects?

82
00:06:17,842 --> 00:06:18,622
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess thoughts.

83
00:06:19,582 --> 00:06:19,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

84
00:06:20,783 --> 00:06:21,683
[SPEAKER_00]: But in between?

85
00:06:22,003 --> 00:06:30,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can have thoughts without needing to seek sensual... What is specifically tied to sensual gratification?

86
00:06:31,465 --> 00:06:34,025
[SPEAKER_00]: What is a necessary phenomenon that needs to be present?

87
00:06:34,045 --> 00:06:36,266
[SPEAKER_00]: To be there for you to chase after.

88
00:06:36,786 --> 00:06:37,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

89
00:06:39,249 --> 00:06:39,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire.

90
00:06:39,870 --> 00:06:40,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire.

91
00:06:40,490 --> 00:06:40,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

92
00:06:41,231 --> 00:06:43,172
[SPEAKER_00]: It's absolutely... And that's the whole thing.

93
00:06:43,913 --> 00:06:53,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if things you're about to do are not necessarily immoral or wrong, if you're doing them out of the desire, they are sensual.

94
00:06:54,682 --> 00:06:54,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

95
00:06:55,023 --> 00:06:58,386
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not engaging them with desire, there's no desire, they're not sensual.

96
00:06:58,586 --> 00:06:58,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

97
00:06:59,246 --> 00:07:03,590
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the desire that determines the context, whether it's the assumption of sensuality or not.

98
00:07:03,990 --> 00:07:06,513
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why... And that's why certain things you cannot do

99
00:07:07,558 --> 00:07:08,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Without desire.

100
00:07:08,399 --> 00:07:08,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

101
00:07:08,979 --> 00:07:14,443
[SPEAKER_01]: That's that's why I, you know, I like to translate karma as sense desire.

102
00:07:15,243 --> 00:07:15,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

103
00:07:15,423 --> 00:07:16,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it comes from the word desire.

104
00:07:16,544 --> 00:07:17,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure, sure.

105
00:07:17,485 --> 00:07:18,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

106
00:07:18,765 --> 00:07:19,366
[SPEAKER_00]: No, absolutely.

107
00:07:19,966 --> 00:07:21,067
[SPEAKER_00]: It is more accurate.

108
00:07:21,627 --> 00:07:23,268
[SPEAKER_00]: So, back on the principle.

109
00:07:27,891 --> 00:07:29,352
[SPEAKER_00]: What parallels can you draw then?

110
00:07:31,374 --> 00:07:34,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire is a necessary basis for sense objects.

111
00:07:34,636 --> 00:07:34,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

112
00:07:35,575 --> 00:07:37,535
[SPEAKER_00]: They are assumed to be pleasant separately.

113
00:07:37,955 --> 00:07:38,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

114
00:07:41,776 --> 00:07:42,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Outside of it.

115
00:07:44,677 --> 00:07:45,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Outside of this?

116
00:07:45,297 --> 00:07:46,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, outside of the desire.

117
00:07:46,877 --> 00:07:47,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Separate to it.

118
00:07:47,697 --> 00:07:48,197
[SPEAKER_00]: After this.

119
00:07:48,437 --> 00:07:48,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

120
00:07:48,917 --> 00:07:49,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever.

121
00:07:52,678 --> 00:07:56,819
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you think that relates then with the thinking and pondering?

122
00:07:58,279 --> 00:08:00,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is, you know, can be quite neutral.

123
00:08:01,880 --> 00:08:02,860
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a routine desire.

124
00:08:03,991 --> 00:08:07,652
[SPEAKER_00]: And the corresponding engagement of speech.

125
00:08:07,912 --> 00:08:08,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Speech.

126
00:08:09,352 --> 00:08:10,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Breaking into speech.

127
00:08:10,613 --> 00:08:15,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Having thought and pondered, one breaks into speech, as the Buddha translated it, as the Buddha said it.

128
00:08:20,455 --> 00:08:22,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that doesn't quite make sense to me right now.

129
00:08:24,653 --> 00:08:26,594
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that would imply...

130
00:08:26,734 --> 00:08:31,778
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm thinking that that would imply that speech is rooted in desire.

131
00:08:32,438 --> 00:08:33,399
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no, no, no, no, no.

132
00:08:33,539 --> 00:08:34,199
[SPEAKER_00]: That can't be right.

133
00:08:34,439 --> 00:08:35,340
[SPEAKER_00]: No, like, listen.

134
00:08:35,540 --> 00:08:36,140
[SPEAKER_00]: I said...

135
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:37,001
[SPEAKER_00]: I said it from the start.

136
00:08:37,641 --> 00:08:40,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire is the necessary basis for sensuality.

137
00:08:40,183 --> 00:08:41,144
[SPEAKER_00]: That's one thing.

138
00:08:41,504 --> 00:08:45,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't mix it now with thinking and pondering and speech.

139
00:08:45,867 --> 00:08:48,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking and pondering is a necessary basis for speech.

140
00:08:50,029 --> 00:08:53,912
[SPEAKER_00]: The principle of desire in sensuality, as in the principle of assumption,

141
00:08:55,116 --> 00:08:57,557
[SPEAKER_00]: How does that compare to this?

142
00:08:57,617 --> 00:09:04,561
[SPEAKER_00]: But not like, oh, so this is... No, I'm not trying to interject it and sort of permutate the members.

143
00:09:04,601 --> 00:09:04,781
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

144
00:09:05,041 --> 00:09:06,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire stays there.

145
00:09:06,242 --> 00:09:08,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking and pondering free from desire stays there.

146
00:09:08,063 --> 00:09:11,184
[SPEAKER_01]: However, there is a certain principle that's in common to both.

147
00:09:11,204 --> 00:09:18,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so desire is the necessary basis for engagement, gratification of a sense of desire.

148
00:09:18,388 --> 00:09:24,751
[SPEAKER_00]: And, incidentally, as you already said it, incidentally, but it was also a right answer as well, just not what I was asking in time,

149
00:09:25,498 --> 00:09:27,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have desire without a thought of desire?

150
00:09:28,380 --> 00:09:28,580
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

151
00:09:28,980 --> 00:09:33,423
[SPEAKER_00]: What is a desire, unless it's your mental thought, an inclination, a craving, an intention?

152
00:09:34,043 --> 00:09:35,644
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's thinking and pondering.

153
00:09:35,764 --> 00:09:38,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Affected with lust, as the Buddha would say, desire is there.

154
00:09:38,906 --> 00:09:41,287
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there can be thinking and pondering with...

155
00:09:41,447 --> 00:09:41,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Without.

156
00:09:41,828 --> 00:09:42,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Without desire.

157
00:09:42,768 --> 00:09:43,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

158
00:09:43,609 --> 00:09:45,650
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as I said, you always have to start...

159
00:09:45,710 --> 00:09:47,091
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, that would be first jhāna.

160
00:09:47,611 --> 00:09:47,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

161
00:09:48,171 --> 00:09:48,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

162
00:09:48,492 --> 00:09:50,153
[SPEAKER_00]: But you only have to start in the reverse order.

163
00:09:50,173 --> 00:09:53,955
[SPEAKER_00]: So, thinking and pondering with desire is the starting point for an unenlightened mind.

164
00:09:54,015 --> 00:09:54,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

165
00:09:55,384 --> 00:09:58,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, thinking and pondering without desire is what you have to develop.

166
00:09:59,308 --> 00:09:59,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

167
00:10:01,530 --> 00:10:07,736
[SPEAKER_00]: And you develop that when you stop engaging with your true, just assumption of separation.

168
00:10:07,756 --> 00:10:09,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Something, something separate.

169
00:10:09,298 --> 00:10:11,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Something external, something outside of you.

170
00:10:11,861 --> 00:10:13,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, to that extent,

171
00:10:13,646 --> 00:10:14,486
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not addressing it.

172
00:10:14,526 --> 00:10:15,907
[SPEAKER_00]: To that extent, it's not on you.

173
00:10:16,027 --> 00:10:17,927
[SPEAKER_00]: To that extent, it's irrelevant.

174
00:10:17,947 --> 00:10:19,187
[SPEAKER_01]: To that extent, it's secondary.

175
00:10:19,247 --> 00:10:20,128
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in the future.

176
00:10:20,248 --> 00:10:21,248
[SPEAKER_01]: It's over there.

177
00:10:21,288 --> 00:10:21,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

178
00:10:21,968 --> 00:10:25,109
[SPEAKER_01]: And to that extent, you remain affected by it.

179
00:10:25,209 --> 00:10:28,810
[SPEAKER_01]: And so that... and that... it's... because it's over there, it implies me.

180
00:10:29,470 --> 00:10:30,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, exactly.

181
00:10:30,090 --> 00:10:31,190
[SPEAKER_00]: It reaffirms this whole thing.

182
00:10:31,250 --> 00:10:31,490
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

183
00:10:31,790 --> 00:10:32,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

184
00:10:33,451 --> 00:10:38,512
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, desire and sensuality, and thinking and pondering without desire, and speech.

185
00:10:40,072 --> 00:10:40,732
[SPEAKER_00]: What are they common?

186
00:10:42,393 --> 00:10:43,513
[SPEAKER_00]: What nature do they share?

187
00:10:46,988 --> 00:10:48,309
[SPEAKER_01]: That one determines the other.

188
00:10:48,569 --> 00:10:52,872
[SPEAKER_01]: That one is the necessary basis for the other.

189
00:10:53,092 --> 00:10:57,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ignorance, say, ignorance of the necessary basis, what is that result?

190
00:10:58,455 --> 00:11:00,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, take the practical example.

191
00:11:00,777 --> 00:11:08,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance, the pleasure you project to be in sense objects, is fully within the context of your desire of it.

192
00:11:08,582 --> 00:11:11,504
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be before, after, not even for a second later.

193
00:11:12,084 --> 00:11:12,985
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always within.

194
00:11:16,716 --> 00:11:20,438
[SPEAKER_00]: You not knowing that, what does that lack of knowledge result in?

195
00:11:21,039 --> 00:11:22,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Chasing after sense objects.

196
00:11:22,900 --> 00:11:26,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would you be chasing after sense objects over there?

197
00:11:26,982 --> 00:11:27,803
[SPEAKER_00]: For the pleasure.

198
00:11:29,183 --> 00:11:30,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you think pleasure is there?

199
00:11:37,108 --> 00:11:38,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you think pleasure is there?

200
00:11:38,329 --> 00:11:39,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's felt as pleasant.

201
00:11:40,650 --> 00:11:43,692
[SPEAKER_00]: These things... Well, no, it's felt as pleasant, but why over there?

202
00:11:45,392 --> 00:11:53,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you're clearly feeling the prospect of sense-object-pleasant, before you get to sense-object, yet you're going to sense-object, but you're feeling already pleasant on account of it.

203
00:11:53,759 --> 00:11:53,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

204
00:11:54,400 --> 00:12:00,125
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why are you going to sense-object, then?

205
00:12:01,867 --> 00:12:04,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you think that's... Because you think it's outside.

206
00:12:04,889 --> 00:12:05,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

207
00:12:05,230 --> 00:12:08,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you think, this pleasure I'm feeling, it's over there.

208
00:12:09,408 --> 00:12:09,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

209
00:12:10,069 --> 00:12:12,711
[SPEAKER_00]: As ridiculous as it sounds, that is in its core of sensuality.

210
00:12:12,791 --> 00:12:14,452
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what's completely gratuitous assumption.

211
00:12:15,073 --> 00:12:26,842
[SPEAKER_00]: And that assumption is the result of you ignoring the fundamental context of necessarily enduring desire for any engagement, even if you are not fully conscious of your desire.

212
00:12:27,963 --> 00:12:34,789
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever you're engaging with sense objects, for the sake of pleasure, there needs to be an underlying container of desire.

213
00:12:35,352 --> 00:12:35,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

214
00:12:35,952 --> 00:12:38,112
[SPEAKER_00]: That it's presently enduring unpleasantly.

215
00:12:38,573 --> 00:12:47,754
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's... knowing that means the gratuitous separation induced by your assumption won't be there anymore, or certainly won't be fueled.

216
00:12:47,914 --> 00:12:48,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

217
00:12:48,254 --> 00:12:51,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing that means this separation is there.

218
00:12:51,195 --> 00:13:01,716
[SPEAKER_01]: And the separation is that... the separation is both that the pleasure is in the thing that I'm chasing after and it's in the future.

219
00:13:02,217 --> 00:13:02,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, of course.

220
00:13:03,097 --> 00:13:04,897
[SPEAKER_00]: It remains in the domain that's always out there.

221
00:13:04,917 --> 00:13:05,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

222
00:13:05,912 --> 00:13:10,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, ironically, even your assumption of separation is within the context of the present-day duty and desire.

223
00:13:10,755 --> 00:13:12,236
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't truly step outside of it.

224
00:13:12,276 --> 00:13:12,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yeah.

225
00:13:13,236 --> 00:13:18,379
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, once you fully understand that, it becomes inconceivable for you to think in those terms anymore.

226
00:13:18,399 --> 00:13:18,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

227
00:13:19,059 --> 00:13:23,242
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as you said then, what unites them is a necessary basis.

228
00:13:24,422 --> 00:13:27,044
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the necessary basis...

229
00:13:27,664 --> 00:13:29,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, what is... what would... what would it result to?

230
00:13:30,506 --> 00:13:33,608
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the necessary basis of thinking and pondering, necessary basis for speech.

231
00:13:34,028 --> 00:13:34,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

232
00:13:36,574 --> 00:13:47,220
[SPEAKER_00]: What would then ignorance of that context, of that order, of that container, what would that result... result to in regard to speech?

233
00:13:50,902 --> 00:13:53,643
[SPEAKER_01]: The speech is somehow separate from your thoughts.

234
00:13:55,625 --> 00:13:56,765
[SPEAKER_00]: In what manner?

235
00:13:56,805 --> 00:13:59,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Describe the characteristics of separation.

236
00:14:19,562 --> 00:14:20,182
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

237
00:14:22,482 --> 00:14:23,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's try this with him.

238
00:14:24,663 --> 00:14:25,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Speech.

239
00:14:25,904 --> 00:14:26,724
[SPEAKER_00]: You're speaking now.

240
00:14:28,765 --> 00:14:35,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that speech independent of the simultaneously presently enduring thinking and pondering behind it?

241
00:14:35,447 --> 00:14:35,607
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

242
00:14:37,813 --> 00:14:39,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that speech before you think and ponder?

243
00:14:39,895 --> 00:14:41,096
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

244
00:14:41,156 --> 00:14:43,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that speech after you've been thinking and pondering?

245
00:14:44,158 --> 00:14:44,378
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

246
00:14:45,739 --> 00:14:50,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the nature of that speech... Not even a nanosecond after thinking and pondering.

247
00:14:50,963 --> 00:14:54,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, but is that speech in its own domain?

248
00:14:59,289 --> 00:15:00,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it has its own domain, yeah.

249
00:15:01,091 --> 00:15:02,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

250
00:15:02,111 --> 00:15:05,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why people then assume separation.

251
00:15:05,354 --> 00:15:05,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

252
00:15:06,539 --> 00:15:08,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not the same as thinking and pondering.

253
00:15:08,540 --> 00:15:09,301
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's not the same.

254
00:15:09,361 --> 00:15:11,402
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see it as a result of your thinking and pondering.

255
00:15:11,422 --> 00:15:15,265
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, you see sensual pursuit as a result of your desire.

256
00:15:16,306 --> 00:15:16,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

257
00:15:16,926 --> 00:15:18,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Aiming at the result of your desire.

258
00:15:19,508 --> 00:15:24,652
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what's in common, as I said, when I asked you initially, to that separation?

259
00:15:24,812 --> 00:15:27,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Assuming outside in regard to the inside.

260
00:15:28,574 --> 00:15:30,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Assuming it to be external.

261
00:15:30,416 --> 00:15:32,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Assuming speech to be external to your thoughts.

262
00:15:32,417 --> 00:15:33,338
[SPEAKER_00]: To thinking and pondering.

263
00:15:33,398 --> 00:15:33,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

264
00:15:34,477 --> 00:15:38,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Which then makes you ignorant of the domain of thinking and pondering.

265
00:15:38,379 --> 00:15:41,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Which then makes you incapable of establishing the first jhāna.

266
00:15:42,201 --> 00:15:42,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that.

267
00:15:44,062 --> 00:15:45,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're putting...

268
00:15:45,402 --> 00:15:53,306
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might be perfectly restrained in doing the centrality, but you still carry on the same principle of the same assumption in regard to the domain of your speech.

269
00:15:53,747 --> 00:15:54,367
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're...

270
00:15:54,387 --> 00:15:58,289
[SPEAKER_00]: So, rather than seeing thinking and pondering through the speech, because that's the only way

271
00:15:59,853 --> 00:16:02,394
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to see your speech within thinking and pondering.

272
00:16:03,434 --> 00:16:03,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just that.

273
00:16:03,974 --> 00:16:07,135
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to see the possibility of your speech within thinking and pondering.

274
00:16:07,295 --> 00:16:11,556
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, you have to see a possibility of your sense desires within the pressure of the desire.

275
00:16:12,977 --> 00:16:13,477
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the thing.

276
00:16:13,817 --> 00:16:18,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, when I'm speaking, then when I'm not speaking, yeah, but there's a possibility to break into speech.

277
00:16:18,818 --> 00:16:19,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

278
00:16:19,238 --> 00:16:20,239
[SPEAKER_00]: The speech domain is always that.

279
00:16:20,259 --> 00:16:23,400
[SPEAKER_00]: And you assume that possibility, at least, to be separate.

280
00:16:23,540 --> 00:16:23,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

281
00:16:24,140 --> 00:16:28,141
[SPEAKER_00]: From the thinking, from the container, to be outside of the container of thinking and pondering.

282
00:16:28,161 --> 00:16:28,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

283
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:35,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you fully understand this, you will be establishing the first jhāna and you will be dwelling in it, as the Buddha would say.

284
00:16:35,762 --> 00:16:40,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're walking, sitting, lying down, going to the toilet, the mind will be in that establishment.

285
00:16:41,063 --> 00:16:42,004
[SPEAKER_00]: So, hang on.

286
00:16:42,024 --> 00:16:42,484
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is...

287
00:16:43,384 --> 00:16:45,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's said the speech ceases.

288
00:16:47,065 --> 00:16:48,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Speech in the first jhāna ceases.

289
00:16:49,026 --> 00:16:49,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

290
00:16:50,986 --> 00:16:52,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in order for you to speak,

291
00:16:53,263 --> 00:16:55,045
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to maintain neutral.

292
00:16:55,585 --> 00:16:57,887
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not an unwholesome assumption like sensuality.

293
00:16:58,007 --> 00:16:58,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

294
00:16:58,587 --> 00:17:01,490
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a neutral assumption of external domain.

295
00:17:01,870 --> 00:17:02,591
[SPEAKER_00]: To the thought.

296
00:17:03,251 --> 00:17:05,733
[SPEAKER_00]: To the thought that you engage with through speaking.

297
00:17:05,814 --> 00:17:06,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

298
00:17:07,095 --> 00:17:09,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And that assumption is like a functional... You step outside the thought.

299
00:17:10,117 --> 00:17:12,939
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to step outside of thought in order to speak.

300
00:17:13,910 --> 00:17:14,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, what happens?

301
00:17:14,811 --> 00:17:20,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Inner mind, stepping outside of the thought, carries the assumption of this is outside of the thought.

302
00:17:21,081 --> 00:17:25,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you... that's why first jhāna is necessary for final knowledge, for arahantship or for being anāgāmī.

303
00:17:26,048 --> 00:17:28,692
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to at least experience it in a sense of

304
00:17:29,250 --> 00:17:30,871
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can... it's possible to speak without

305
00:17:58,232 --> 00:17:59,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Without assuming.

306
00:17:59,153 --> 00:18:02,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Without assuming the extent, without giving it a separate identity.

307
00:18:03,537 --> 00:18:10,863
[SPEAKER_00]: It's possible to speak without losing the context of the necessary container, necessary basis of thinking and pondering as a domain.

308
00:18:11,664 --> 00:18:15,807
[SPEAKER_00]: But even then, you have to turn away from thinking and pondering in order to speak.

309
00:18:16,908 --> 00:18:19,591
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to break into speech, as the Buddha would say.

310
00:18:21,352 --> 00:18:22,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the first jhāna,

311
00:18:22,636 --> 00:18:34,719
[SPEAKER_00]: You get to, like, surmount that, so that you understand, or rather it would then result in your understanding, that that does not require an assumption of an external world where the speech occurs.

312
00:18:35,079 --> 00:18:50,242
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you say that... although, right now, you're not speaking, speech is there, the domain of speech is there,

313
00:18:52,078 --> 00:18:55,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's always the possibility for you to break out into speech.

314
00:18:56,201 --> 00:18:57,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but more accurately...

315
00:18:59,052 --> 00:19:07,495
[SPEAKER_00]: That possibility is also there, as a possibility, because the mind hasn't been established on the domain of thinking and pondering properly enough.

316
00:19:07,915 --> 00:19:08,835
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I'm saying.

317
00:19:08,855 --> 00:19:13,296
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the possibility of speech is not the same thing as thinking and pondering.

318
00:19:13,696 --> 00:19:14,257
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

319
00:19:14,417 --> 00:19:16,837
[SPEAKER_00]: It's within... everything is within thinking and pondering.

320
00:19:16,857 --> 00:19:19,158
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the certain thoughts and certain ponders.

321
00:19:19,498 --> 00:19:19,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

322
00:19:19,898 --> 00:19:20,959
[SPEAKER_00]: They can break into speech.

323
00:19:21,799 --> 00:19:25,400
[SPEAKER_00]: They can turn away from its own nature in order to speak, in order to express it.

324
00:19:25,740 --> 00:19:34,143
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's just the same as saying nothing can be experienced apart from mentally, as a kind of mental phenomenon.

325
00:19:34,163 --> 00:19:39,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, nothing can be experienced outside of the container of mental phenomena.

326
00:19:39,165 --> 00:19:39,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

327
00:19:40,366 --> 00:19:45,328
[SPEAKER_00]: There are different aspects within that, but there is nothing separate to it, as in external to it.

328
00:19:45,408 --> 00:19:45,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

329
00:19:47,362 --> 00:19:55,528
[SPEAKER_01]: So, thinking about pure matter presents itself as a thought.

330
00:19:55,608 --> 00:19:58,970
[SPEAKER_01]: But you could say that within this domain of thinking and pondering.

331
00:20:01,112 --> 00:20:01,332
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

332
00:20:02,052 --> 00:20:08,617
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, if you develop thinking and pondering rightly, if you develop first jhāna, you are free from conceivings.

333
00:20:08,657 --> 00:20:16,923
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you had any understanding of our principle of conceivings and upādāna, there would be more than enough for you to fully understand it and free yourself entirely.

334
00:20:23,070 --> 00:20:28,974
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the speech ceases, because you saw it as fully contained.

335
00:20:28,994 --> 00:20:35,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how far, how long, how loud you might be speaking, how much you have been speaking, how much you will be speaking.

336
00:20:35,779 --> 00:20:35,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

337
00:20:36,200 --> 00:20:46,547
[SPEAKER_00]: It can never step outside of the domain of thinking and pondering required to be there enduring simultaneously with your speech, but also required for you to not think about it in order to speak.

338
00:20:48,909 --> 00:20:50,030
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you break into speech.

339
00:20:50,050 --> 00:20:50,510
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't be...

340
00:20:50,798 --> 00:20:53,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking about what you're speaking and speaking.

341
00:20:53,220 --> 00:20:55,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Your speaking becomes your thinking about what you're speaking.

342
00:20:55,342 --> 00:20:55,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

343
00:20:56,683 --> 00:20:58,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why you turn away from thinking about...

344
00:20:58,344 --> 00:20:59,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why it's neutral.

345
00:20:59,986 --> 00:21:15,078
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no problem with it, but there is a problem for somebody who hasn't been freed from assumptions, from upādāna, because any principle of of putting second as first, then first as second, is the principle that is then maintaining the fundamental ignorance.

346
00:21:19,587 --> 00:21:25,028
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to learn how to speak without putting the speech first and thought second.

347
00:21:25,048 --> 00:21:25,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

348
00:21:26,989 --> 00:21:27,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Without forgetting.

349
00:21:28,309 --> 00:21:30,030
[SPEAKER_00]: And without refusing to speak ever again.

350
00:21:30,170 --> 00:21:30,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

351
00:21:30,850 --> 00:21:33,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then you're still within the possibility of speaking.

352
00:21:33,191 --> 00:21:35,071
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's going to be even harder for you to understand it.

353
00:21:35,091 --> 00:21:35,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

354
00:21:36,512 --> 00:21:38,532
[SPEAKER_01]: But, but...

355
00:21:38,552 --> 00:21:43,093
[SPEAKER_01]: So, without kind of losing sight of the necessary basis for that speech.

356
00:21:45,774 --> 00:21:48,915
[SPEAKER_00]: So, speech ceases when you became

357
00:21:50,482 --> 00:22:19,884
[SPEAKER_00]: When you develop full clarity in regard to the nature of speech being always within the container of thinking and pondering, you dwell on that newly clarified context long enough, at least for that time, as a result of your dwelling on that imperturbable nature, imperturbable order, you become unable to sort of even direct yourself, so to speak, towards possibility of speech.

358
00:22:20,963 --> 00:22:23,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the actual speech has ceased.

359
00:22:25,204 --> 00:22:26,565
[SPEAKER_00]: The actual speech has ceased.

360
00:22:26,605 --> 00:22:30,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the speech is there when you are within the possibilities of saying something.

361
00:22:31,006 --> 00:22:31,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

362
00:22:33,526 --> 00:22:41,349
[SPEAKER_00]: But whether you are saying something, whether you are abstaining from speaking, whether there are possibilities, all that is within the presently enduring thinking and pondering.

363
00:22:44,020 --> 00:23:00,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for you now, you might say, okay, my thinking and pondering is limited, but the possibilities of speaking, you still kind of gratuitously assume as belonging to the speech domain, which you gratuitously assume to be external, not as if belonging to the thinking and pondering domain, which is neither external nor internal.

364
00:23:00,350 --> 00:23:01,030
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just there.

365
00:23:01,050 --> 00:23:01,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

366
00:23:02,732 --> 00:23:07,354
[SPEAKER_00]: External... Internal becomes defined when you externalize things with your gratuitous assumptions.

367
00:23:07,414 --> 00:23:07,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

368
00:23:07,875 --> 00:23:10,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, in return, they're defined, which is not external.

369
00:23:10,236 --> 00:23:11,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, that's my internal.

370
00:23:11,737 --> 00:23:11,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Me.

371
00:23:12,498 --> 00:23:13,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Me, exactly.

372
00:23:15,637 --> 00:23:17,297
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is the principle for all the jhānas.

373
00:23:19,378 --> 00:23:20,838
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why... Is there more than this?

374
00:23:21,478 --> 00:23:24,359
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the second jhāna, thinking and pondering would cease.

375
00:23:25,159 --> 00:23:25,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

376
00:23:25,419 --> 00:23:33,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Because thinking and pondering has been assumed external in regard to what's then in the second jhāna, what's the necessary basis for it.

377
00:23:34,501 --> 00:23:36,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Joy, pity...

378
00:23:36,622 --> 00:23:36,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

379
00:23:37,002 --> 00:23:39,542
[SPEAKER_00]: A certain agreeable... Happiness.

380
00:23:39,562 --> 00:23:42,603
[SPEAKER_00]: ...economity, sort of agreeability of it.

381
00:23:44,321 --> 00:23:45,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is not much movement.

382
00:23:45,902 --> 00:23:50,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Same principle then goes to the immaterial establishments of the mind.

383
00:23:51,205 --> 00:23:53,906
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the... so, which... Like, for example, breathing stops.

384
00:23:54,687 --> 00:23:54,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

385
00:23:55,327 --> 00:23:56,007
[SPEAKER_00]: In the four jhāna.

386
00:23:56,568 --> 00:23:57,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Because... Why?

387
00:23:57,568 --> 00:24:02,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you do assume movement of the breath to be external to the body.

388
00:24:03,131 --> 00:24:03,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

389
00:24:03,692 --> 00:24:06,053
[SPEAKER_00]: You... tiny bit, you still assume it.

390
00:24:06,273 --> 00:24:06,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

391
00:24:06,653 --> 00:24:11,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that... how can they even... how can any notion of breath even be conceivable?

392
00:24:12,033 --> 00:24:14,415
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's not defined entirely within the body.

393
00:24:15,876 --> 00:24:20,320
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the subtlest of assumptions are the ones like third jhāna, fourth jhāna.

394
00:24:20,700 --> 00:24:32,350
[SPEAKER_00]: But as I said, it's assumption in its nature of assuming external while it's actually within it.

395
00:24:33,070 --> 00:24:39,956
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not assumption necessarily in the nature of containing ignorance and lust and aversion.

396
00:24:40,768 --> 00:24:41,268
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as long as...

397
00:24:41,288 --> 00:24:44,069
[SPEAKER_00]: I just want to clarify that so people don't go, ah, assumption, assumption.

398
00:24:44,529 --> 00:24:48,231
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, as I said, theoretically, Puthujana can develop jhānas.

399
00:24:48,291 --> 00:24:48,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I see what you mean.

400
00:24:49,011 --> 00:24:51,752
[SPEAKER_00]: And still remain ignorant of the fundamental nature.

401
00:24:51,812 --> 00:24:52,672
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm used to the word assumption.

402
00:24:52,852 --> 00:24:53,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

403
00:24:53,192 --> 00:24:53,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

404
00:24:53,873 --> 00:24:59,634
[SPEAKER_00]: But if he... Like, if Puthujana develops jhāna, you could see how much work he already done.

405
00:24:59,835 --> 00:25:00,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

406
00:25:00,335 --> 00:25:03,696
[SPEAKER_00]: He already done all these structural assumptions he understood.

