00:35:15,265 --> 00:35:22,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I don't know, separate from... What is that in its nature, the notion of outside?

619
00:35:23,769 --> 00:35:25,270
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you even talk about it?

620
00:35:25,570 --> 00:35:26,170
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

621
00:35:33,474 --> 00:35:33,854
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

622
00:35:34,734 --> 00:35:39,297
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... outside, it's like...

623
00:35:39,657 --> 00:35:41,778
[SPEAKER_01]: The notion, any notion, what is it in its nature?

624
00:35:42,718 --> 00:35:43,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's... it's... it's...

625
00:35:45,635 --> 00:35:46,375
[SPEAKER_01]: It's manifested.

626
00:35:46,636 --> 00:35:46,996
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that.

627
00:35:47,116 --> 00:35:48,517
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically... What is it in its nature?

628
00:35:49,637 --> 00:35:50,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Not what it's doing.

629
00:35:50,658 --> 00:35:50,978
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

630
00:35:52,539 --> 00:35:53,880
[SPEAKER_00]: A notion of this, a notion of that.

631
00:35:53,900 --> 00:35:54,240
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

632
00:35:57,022 --> 00:35:57,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, any notion.

633
00:35:58,142 --> 00:35:58,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Any notion.

634
00:35:58,843 --> 00:36:00,103
[SPEAKER_00]: What is a notion in its nature?

635
00:36:00,204 --> 00:36:00,584
[SPEAKER_00]: A thought.

636
00:36:00,764 --> 00:36:01,524
[SPEAKER_00]: A thought.

637
00:36:01,925 --> 00:36:03,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

638
00:36:03,886 --> 00:36:09,349
[SPEAKER_00]: So, within the... which is already thinking and pondering, possibility of speech is already a thought.

639
00:36:09,369 --> 00:36:09,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

640
00:36:09,969 --> 00:36:12,511
[SPEAKER_00]: You have another thought within that thought.

641
00:36:12,651 --> 00:36:12,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

642
00:36:13,551 --> 00:36:15,592
[SPEAKER_00]: That says that I'm not a thought.

643
00:36:15,692 --> 00:36:18,154
[SPEAKER_01]: I am outside of these thoughts.

644
00:36:18,174 --> 00:36:18,534
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

645
00:36:19,254 --> 00:36:22,977
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your notion of outside is an inside thought, so to speak.

646
00:36:24,537 --> 00:36:26,038
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why there is an assumption.

647
00:36:26,118 --> 00:36:37,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because of speech, not because of the principle of breaking into speech, turning away from... You can turn away from thoughts all you want, and speak all you want, and still not assume the thought to contradict itself.

648
00:36:38,546 --> 00:36:42,588
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, this is outside, and I can only think that on the inside.

649
00:36:44,753 --> 00:36:47,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can be free from assumptions and still speak.

650
00:36:47,855 --> 00:36:48,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Arhat wouldn't go mute.

651
00:36:49,116 --> 00:36:49,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

652
00:36:50,978 --> 00:36:52,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

653
00:36:52,839 --> 00:37:05,368
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not dwelling long enough on the characteristic of outside within your thoughts, you gratuitously maintain the notion of outside that's then carried and perpetuated with your speech that's affected by ignorance.

654
00:37:06,829 --> 00:37:08,711
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, this

655
00:37:13,577 --> 00:37:18,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Speech... the possibility of speech is being the possibility of stepping outside.

656
00:37:19,401 --> 00:37:21,483
[SPEAKER_01]: It's based upon this... No.

657
00:37:21,703 --> 00:37:22,483
[SPEAKER_00]: You should say like this.

658
00:37:22,904 --> 00:37:26,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Possibility of speech is possibility of turning away from thoughts.

659
00:37:26,406 --> 00:37:26,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

660
00:37:28,407 --> 00:37:29,388
[SPEAKER_00]: But not stepping outside.

661
00:37:31,630 --> 00:37:35,953
[SPEAKER_00]: With ignorance, turning away from thoughts means being outside.

662
00:37:36,714 --> 00:37:38,455
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, outside of what?

663
00:37:38,535 --> 00:37:40,016
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, well, that's already a thought.

664
00:37:40,036 --> 00:37:41,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't be truly outside.

665
00:37:41,376 --> 00:37:41,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's what...

666
00:37:41,877 --> 00:37:45,498
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only reason you're outside is because you don't fully understand you can't be outside.

667
00:37:45,518 --> 00:37:45,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

668
00:37:46,259 --> 00:37:47,199
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm trying to say.

669
00:37:47,799 --> 00:37:51,781
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

670
00:37:54,523 --> 00:37:57,744
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there can be speech without assumption.

671
00:37:58,945 --> 00:37:59,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

672
00:37:59,945 --> 00:38:02,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you now... you see...

673
00:38:05,320 --> 00:38:25,205
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you break out into speech, but that breaking out into speech cannot take you outside of the domain of thinking and pondering, because the whole domain of thinking and pondering is completely enclosed within... the whole domain of speech and the possibility of speech is enclosed within thinking and pondering.

674
00:38:25,705 --> 00:38:30,767
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, all of your physical actions are enclosed within your mental intention.

675
00:38:32,205 --> 00:38:34,907
[SPEAKER_00]: All your verbal actions are enclosed within mental intentions.

676
00:38:38,049 --> 00:38:49,996
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you want to take a thought of outside, allow it to endure, as that which says it's outside, and see the whole thing within the nature of being a thought.

677
00:38:51,937 --> 00:38:55,660
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you become incapable of assuming.

678
00:38:58,112 --> 00:39:00,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Of maintaining upādāna, of perverting the order.

679
00:39:00,733 --> 00:39:08,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you realize that every single single example that could possibly arise of going outside, can't possibly be outside.

680
00:39:08,814 --> 00:39:09,695
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's first jhāna.

681
00:39:10,315 --> 00:39:13,175
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I said it many times, and I'll keep saying it.

682
00:39:14,676 --> 00:39:24,658
[SPEAKER_00]: With Mahāmūgālāna, when the Buddha encouraged him to practice jhāna, which means he didn't have jhānas at the time, when the Buddha left, he sat under a tree and said, yeah, first jhāna, first jhāna.

683
00:39:24,678 --> 00:39:25,498
[SPEAKER_00]: What is first jhāna?

684
00:39:26,115 --> 00:39:27,416
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't you get it by just staring at your nostrils?

685
00:39:41,362 --> 00:39:42,103
[SPEAKER_00]: I think not.

686
00:39:42,263 --> 00:39:50,908
[SPEAKER_00]: I think the Buddha would have said, instead of dwelling on these factors, characteristics of the first jhāna, why don't you just watch your nostrils, and it will happen to you.

687
00:39:51,448 --> 00:39:58,152
[SPEAKER_00]: The overwhelming experience of joy you're going to have, then somebody will tell you, that was the jhāna, and then you will know it was the jhāna.

688
00:39:58,933 --> 00:40:00,154
[SPEAKER_01]: But you weren't conscious at the time.

689
00:40:00,174 --> 00:40:03,796
[SPEAKER_00]: You were kind of... You might have been slightly out.

690
00:40:04,716 --> 00:40:10,260
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is, it needs to be understood, or at least recognized,

691
00:40:10,837 --> 00:40:14,900
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have a possibility to utter words with your mouth, and that's fine.

692
00:40:15,141 --> 00:40:23,507
[SPEAKER_00]: However, if you're ignorant of the nature of that possibility, the nature of it being in us,

693
00:40:37,071 --> 00:40:54,945
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature with being a saṅkhara, the necessary basis, the nature with being a container, that it cannot be stepped outside, then you committing to uttering words with your mouth, is then you assuming somehow pertaining to this external domain that is separate from

694
00:40:55,792 --> 00:41:00,934
[SPEAKER_00]: The thinking and pondering of the possibility of the speech, that was there throughout, has to be there.

695
00:41:01,654 --> 00:41:06,796
[SPEAKER_00]: If you suddenly stop thinking what you were about to say or what you were saying, you will stop speaking.

696
00:41:06,816 --> 00:41:07,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

697
00:41:09,116 --> 00:41:17,159
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so it's why samādhi, jhāna, is called unification of mind.

698
00:41:17,399 --> 00:41:17,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

699
00:41:18,199 --> 00:41:19,440
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's called imperturbable.

700
00:41:21,307 --> 00:41:25,070
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not imperturbable in some majestic, mystical, transcendental sense.

701
00:41:25,610 --> 00:41:31,175
[SPEAKER_00]: It's imperturbable because you have restored the right order that is now imperturbable.

702
00:41:31,215 --> 00:41:34,117
[SPEAKER_00]: The definition of... like, turn something upside down.

703
00:41:35,558 --> 00:41:37,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, now it's unturnable.

704
00:41:38,094 --> 00:41:43,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind got established upon the right order and saw it as... And saw it as not optional.

705
00:41:43,699 --> 00:41:44,260
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

706
00:41:44,740 --> 00:41:46,262
[SPEAKER_00]: The wrong order was established.

707
00:41:46,282 --> 00:41:49,585
[SPEAKER_00]: The perversions were there because you didn't realize they can't be there.

708
00:41:50,225 --> 00:41:51,987
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the basis for them being there.

709
00:41:52,327 --> 00:41:55,370
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you are responsible for their bhāva, for their being.

710
00:41:56,611 --> 00:41:58,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's fully assumed by you.

711
00:41:58,413 --> 00:42:03,018
[SPEAKER_01]: But can you say as well, because it begins with thinking and pondering, this mental domain as being...

712
00:42:04,237 --> 00:42:08,039
[SPEAKER_01]: That necessary base, that container, that nothing can step outside of.

713
00:42:08,159 --> 00:42:08,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

714
00:42:08,640 --> 00:42:10,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Even any assumption of stepping outside of it.

715
00:42:10,140 --> 00:42:11,841
[SPEAKER_00]: That's enough for the complete liberation.

716
00:42:12,021 --> 00:42:13,002
[SPEAKER_00]: First jhāna.

717
00:42:13,142 --> 00:42:15,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding the extent of that domain.

718
00:42:15,303 --> 00:42:16,964
[SPEAKER_00]: That's unification of mind.

719
00:42:17,104 --> 00:42:23,288
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more than enough for a full uprooting of all this, all the like, lust, aversion, delusion, all the suffering.

720
00:42:27,375 --> 00:42:35,962
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't remember the sutra, but people can maybe mention in the comments, if they know the reference, where the Buddha says that... that... for the first...

721
00:42:35,982 --> 00:42:36,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one?

722
00:42:37,163 --> 00:42:47,290
[SPEAKER_00]: The first jhāna is... something like... Yeah, but he says that anāgāmī, being free from sensuality or final liberation, are dependent on the first jhāna.

723
00:42:47,310 --> 00:42:47,931
[SPEAKER_00]: On the first jhāna.

724
00:42:48,051 --> 00:42:49,772
[SPEAKER_00]: And they're undependent on the second jhāna.

725
00:42:49,993 --> 00:42:50,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Or higher.

726
00:42:50,653 --> 00:42:51,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

727
00:42:52,414 --> 00:42:55,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you can get the right view and everything else before that.

728
00:42:58,301 --> 00:43:11,665
[SPEAKER_00]: But great, if you get it on the basis of first jhāna, there is very little work for you to do between being a sotāpanna and an arahant, if you already have developed mind to this imperturbable order of the nature of things.

729
00:43:12,005 --> 00:43:13,265
[SPEAKER_00]: What comes first is first.

730
00:43:16,526 --> 00:43:17,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, isn't it marvelous?

731
00:43:17,626 --> 00:43:22,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Putting second that which I was putting first, and putting first that which I was putting second.

732
00:43:22,868 --> 00:43:23,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Fascinating.

733
00:43:24,188 --> 00:43:25,569
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't create anything, by the way.

734
00:43:25,709 --> 00:43:27,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Even arahant can't create anything.

735
00:43:27,630 --> 00:43:28,730
[SPEAKER_00]: It just undoes the mistake.

736
00:43:29,651 --> 00:43:31,751
[SPEAKER_00]: The perversion of the order that was completely gratuitous.

737
00:43:32,632 --> 00:43:34,212
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't actually change the order.

738
00:43:34,232 --> 00:43:38,774
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't actually have the external domain of your speech independent of your thought.

739
00:43:38,814 --> 00:43:40,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, that's how you live.

740
00:43:40,134 --> 00:43:44,956
[SPEAKER_00]: And for eons and eons and eons, because of your gratuitous assumption.

741
00:43:45,656 --> 00:43:46,797
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you approve the assumption.

742
00:43:47,217 --> 00:43:47,477
[SPEAKER_00]: You have

743
00:43:48,311 --> 00:43:54,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing the maker of the raft and the rafters are destroyed and the maker of the house, as we were saying.

744
00:43:54,994 --> 00:43:55,794
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the maker.

745
00:43:55,914 --> 00:43:56,714
[SPEAKER_00]: All these assumptions.

746
00:43:56,834 --> 00:43:57,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

747
00:43:57,434 --> 00:44:06,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Gratuitous, little, assuming external in regard to this, giving it a separate identity, which in return defines you as an identity, and so on.

748
00:44:07,918 --> 00:44:09,439
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, once you stop,

749
00:44:11,210 --> 00:44:18,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Assuming something external, then... How do you stop assuming something external?

750
00:44:18,776 --> 00:44:21,419
[SPEAKER_01]: By including whatever it is.

751
00:44:22,039 --> 00:44:30,827
[SPEAKER_01]: If any thought, any notion, any kind of something external, by recognizing that that has to be included within

752
00:44:31,724 --> 00:44:32,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Really what?

753
00:44:32,945 --> 00:44:34,086
[SPEAKER_01]: The necessary basis.

754
00:44:34,547 --> 00:44:35,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what?

755
00:44:35,808 --> 00:44:39,692
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this case, what you're talking about is thinking and pondering on the mind.

756
00:44:39,832 --> 00:44:40,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

757
00:44:40,213 --> 00:44:41,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Within the...

758
00:44:41,154 --> 00:44:44,217
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to say within the mind, because everything is in my mind.

759
00:44:44,257 --> 00:44:45,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah, yeah, yeah.

760
00:44:45,057 --> 00:44:47,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Which kind of implies, oh, then there are other things that are outside of your mind.

761
00:44:48,060 --> 00:44:48,321
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

762
00:44:48,821 --> 00:44:54,187
[SPEAKER_00]: But the thought of outside of your mind, well, that's a thought which means it's within my mind, which means it's truly inconceivable.

763
00:44:55,194 --> 00:45:04,360
[SPEAKER_00]: When I think outside, or inside, or both, or neither, or in between, can all be done on the basis of the mind being there, the container of it, the container of the thought.

764
00:45:04,721 --> 00:45:08,123
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why I've just put mind up was because I was thinking Samādhi is the unification of mind.

765
00:45:08,263 --> 00:45:08,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

766
00:45:09,364 --> 00:45:09,844
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, yeah.

767
00:45:10,144 --> 00:45:17,690
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you see, I think the same principle can also be done, I think, like we're talking in the last talk, about the body.

768
00:45:18,030 --> 00:45:18,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

769
00:45:19,311 --> 00:45:21,592
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can choose... That's why there are all those different

770
00:45:24,075 --> 00:45:27,437
[SPEAKER_01]: The commentator is translating objects that you can use to develop first jhāna.

771
00:45:27,497 --> 00:45:27,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

772
00:45:28,057 --> 00:45:28,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

773
00:45:28,457 --> 00:45:29,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Kammatthana.

774
00:45:29,538 --> 00:45:29,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

775
00:45:30,438 --> 00:45:31,158
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not objects.

776
00:45:31,178 --> 00:45:36,661
[SPEAKER_00]: They're literally the same things that you can understand, the same principle, that you can establish the same right order.

777
00:45:37,221 --> 00:45:38,542
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where jhāna will be then.

778
00:45:43,644 --> 00:45:44,544
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, the body.

779
00:45:44,704 --> 00:45:45,585
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can use the body.

780
00:45:45,625 --> 00:45:47,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Assuming outside of the body.

781
00:45:47,345 --> 00:45:48,146
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you know that?

782
00:45:49,788 --> 00:45:57,434
[SPEAKER_00]: As I asked you in the previous talk, when I recorded the kāramī yesterday before, well, when you're seeing things out there, like, distantly... Yeah.

783
00:45:58,535 --> 00:46:00,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how could that... how could that thing...

784
00:46:00,236 --> 00:46:01,617
[SPEAKER_01]: If the eye is not...

785
00:46:01,657 --> 00:46:02,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

786
00:46:02,037 --> 00:46:03,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Necessary basis, that's right here.

787
00:46:03,478 --> 00:46:05,160
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, you're seeing the eye seeing things.

788
00:46:05,380 --> 00:46:05,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

789
00:46:06,100 --> 00:46:13,766
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only relationship you can have with the world outside of your senses is the relationship that gets mirrored onto your senses.

790
00:46:13,926 --> 00:46:14,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

791
00:46:14,107 --> 00:46:16,348
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't see things that mirror things onto your senses.

792
00:46:16,368 --> 00:46:18,430
[SPEAKER_00]: You only see the mirror things on your senses.

793
00:46:18,450 --> 00:46:18,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

794
00:46:18,943 --> 00:46:26,192
[SPEAKER_00]: You only see the senses affected by these things, and then you assume those are the things outside of these senses, and that's inconceivable.

795
00:46:26,212 --> 00:46:28,976
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you conceive matter, or misconceive.

796
00:46:29,676 --> 00:46:35,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Any form of conceiving outside, as in conceiving the elements, conceiving the matter, as the Buddha said, it's misconceiving.

797
00:46:37,325 --> 00:46:43,087
[SPEAKER_01]: And so that's... that applies to any notion of something far away over there, outside of me.

798
00:46:43,107 --> 00:46:44,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Any notion of space or distance.

799
00:46:44,247 --> 00:46:46,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Or... or into... also in terms of time.

800
00:46:46,328 --> 00:46:46,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Time, yeah.

801
00:46:46,848 --> 00:46:51,869
[SPEAKER_01]: In terms of the future, which is... which I'm worried about, I'm thinking about that, because that's over there.

802
00:46:51,889 --> 00:46:52,249
[SPEAKER_01]: It's old, again.

803
00:46:52,410 --> 00:46:53,010
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not here.

804
00:46:53,270 --> 00:46:54,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Does that... and that's what we're saying.

805
00:46:54,710 --> 00:47:03,773
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't... let's say... it doesn't matter if it's before, later, eh... old, new, big, small, up, down.

806
00:47:04,888 --> 00:47:10,109
[SPEAKER_00]: matter, or feeling, or intention, or perception, matter is there, feeling is there.

807
00:47:10,690 --> 00:47:11,870
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, I have thoughts of past.

808
00:47:11,890 --> 00:47:13,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, stop thinking, stop thinking.

809
00:47:13,310 --> 00:47:14,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's another thought.

810
00:47:14,271 --> 00:47:14,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

811
00:47:14,851 --> 00:47:19,552
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you say, you think you stop thinking while you're thinking, and you're actually deluding yourself even further.

812
00:47:19,652 --> 00:47:19,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

813
00:47:20,172 --> 00:47:22,633
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you think about past, you plan about the future.

814
00:47:23,533 --> 00:47:30,235
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you do that in any other way, except thinking, right here, right now, presently, right here, right now?

815
00:47:30,275 --> 00:47:30,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

816
00:47:31,190 --> 00:47:32,030
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not optional.

817
00:47:32,190 --> 00:47:36,971
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, instead of you trying to interfere with it and prevent it, I must stop planning or something.

818
00:47:37,512 --> 00:47:42,373
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you must stop acting carelessly out of your plans, but that's a completely separate domain of you choosing to act.

819
00:47:42,733 --> 00:47:46,154
[SPEAKER_00]: But thoughts of your plans, thoughts of your past, are thoughts.

820
00:47:47,214 --> 00:47:48,674
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all you're concerned about.

821
00:47:48,734 --> 00:47:50,975
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't care the content of your thoughts.

822
00:47:51,875 --> 00:47:53,916
[SPEAKER_00]: You care about... it's a thought.

823
00:47:54,656 --> 00:47:55,036
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

824
00:47:55,136 --> 00:47:56,056
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the thought is there.

825
00:47:57,176 --> 00:47:58,957
[SPEAKER_00]: If the thought is there, anything that

826
00:47:59,971 --> 00:48:04,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Is in response to that thought, cannot be outside of the container of that thought.

827
00:48:04,973 --> 00:48:06,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence the intentionality behind your actions.

828
00:48:09,015 --> 00:48:12,056
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like one of the most fundamental and most practical things you can develop.

829
00:48:12,797 --> 00:48:15,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't commit to full-on practicing the dhamma.

830
00:48:16,158 --> 00:48:22,461
[SPEAKER_00]: It will result in, like, responsibility, transparency, confidence, strength and virtue, wisdom.

831
00:48:22,501 --> 00:48:23,421
[SPEAKER_00]: It will result in wisdom.

832
00:48:25,482 --> 00:48:27,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you stop perverting the order.

833
00:48:27,907 --> 00:48:29,648
[SPEAKER_00]: On the level of body, speech and mind.

834
00:48:30,529 --> 00:48:31,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's unpervertable.

835
00:48:33,871 --> 00:48:34,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that.

836
00:48:34,792 --> 00:48:35,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that?

837
00:48:35,572 --> 00:48:37,254
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your perversion, yeah.

838
00:48:37,974 --> 00:48:39,315
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you just keep acting out of it.

839
00:48:45,159 --> 00:48:56,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, including the characteristic of outside, of this possible... of these thoughts of possibility of speaking while I'm not speaking, that's how you cease to speak.

840
00:49:00,050 --> 00:49:18,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Including the characteristic of outside, characteristic that is sort of bundled with, that is sort of implied in possibility of breaking into speech, possibility to break into outside.

841
00:49:18,503 --> 00:49:24,267
[SPEAKER_00]: That last bit of outside, you include as within the domain of thinking and pondering.

842
00:49:25,464 --> 00:49:26,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Develop that.

843
00:49:26,325 --> 00:49:27,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Develop clarity in regard to that.

844
00:49:28,388 --> 00:49:30,271
[SPEAKER_00]: At that time, that's the first jhāna.

845
00:49:31,152 --> 00:49:34,197
[SPEAKER_00]: At that time, your sees... your speech has ceased.

846
00:49:35,673 --> 00:49:48,121
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you go and break into... You're speechless in the sense that... Well, in the sense that... You're... you're... you now... any possibility to speak does not involve an assumption of going outside... Outside, yeah.

847
00:49:48,141 --> 00:49:49,062
[SPEAKER_00]: ...of the thinking and pondering.

848
00:49:49,102 --> 00:49:49,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

849
00:49:49,662 --> 00:49:54,866
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then... then you might wonder, so, how can you speak ever again, afterwards, once you've undone the assumption?

850
00:49:55,106 --> 00:49:55,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

851
00:49:55,406 --> 00:49:59,349
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can still speak without changing that assumption, without kind of... That's what I mean.

852
00:49:59,529 --> 00:50:01,791
[SPEAKER_00]: You assuming outside is an extra step.

853
00:50:02,011 --> 00:50:02,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

854
00:50:02,651 --> 00:50:02,991
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

855
00:50:03,011 --> 00:50:03,492
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

856
00:50:04,468 --> 00:50:11,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Assuming outside is an extension of the looking away from the thoughts towards the speech.

857
00:50:11,572 --> 00:50:12,092
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's it.

858
00:50:12,112 --> 00:50:13,233
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where it ends.

859
00:50:13,313 --> 00:50:14,233
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's hard to see.

860
00:50:14,633 --> 00:50:18,775
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just naturally assume a better ending, which is outside world.

861
00:50:20,056 --> 00:50:22,918
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you're responsible for it, because it's not there.

862
00:50:24,158 --> 00:50:29,121
[SPEAKER_00]: So, turning away from thoughts in order to speak is a necessary basis for speech.

863
00:50:29,401 --> 00:50:31,282
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a necessary action behind the speech.

864
00:50:32,373 --> 00:50:35,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Turning away from thoughts with ignorance assumes the are.

865
00:50:36,202 --> 00:50:40,051
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm turning away from thoughts from here, I'm turning over there.

866
00:50:42,389 --> 00:50:48,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you stop going that far, you will still be able to speak without assuming external world of speech.

867
00:50:48,872 --> 00:50:49,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

868
00:50:49,912 --> 00:50:54,635
[SPEAKER_01]: You are temporarily looking away from the thinking and pondering.

869
00:50:55,215 --> 00:50:56,095
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the nature of speech.

870
00:50:56,175 --> 00:50:57,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Breaking into speech.

871
00:50:57,216 --> 00:50:58,256
[SPEAKER_01]: But...

872
00:50:58,276 --> 00:51:00,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with body action, by the way.

873
00:51:00,277 --> 00:51:04,099
[SPEAKER_01]: But that turning away doesn't involve a kind of...

874
00:51:04,239 --> 00:51:09,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Assuming the separate domain where this turning away will occur, because that's contradiction in terms.

875
00:51:11,399 --> 00:51:14,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all you need to do is approve the assumption from it.

876
00:51:14,022 --> 00:51:32,641
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do... why is this assumption of outside so strong, so alluring, so... obviously because there's some...

877
00:51:33,667 --> 00:51:36,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure involved in the assumption of something.

878
00:51:36,408 --> 00:51:36,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course.

879
00:51:37,108 --> 00:51:40,650
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I can see now... Well, there's a pleasure in the nature of upādāna.

880
00:51:41,070 --> 00:51:41,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

881
00:51:41,290 --> 00:51:43,491
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter necessarily the content.

882
00:51:43,871 --> 00:51:48,412
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, if it's a upādāna about the speech or upādāna about self-view.

883
00:51:48,452 --> 00:51:49,753
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's about the outsideness.

884
00:51:49,793 --> 00:51:50,833
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I'm trying to get at.

885
00:51:50,893 --> 00:51:51,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Outside-ness.

886
00:51:51,593 --> 00:51:52,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

887
00:51:52,714 --> 00:51:57,015
[SPEAKER_01]: There's something about externality, which in itself is pleasant.

888
00:51:57,215 --> 00:51:57,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

889
00:51:57,916 --> 00:51:58,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Because

890
00:51:59,256 --> 00:52:01,257
[SPEAKER_01]: It's beyond this pain.

891
00:52:02,038 --> 00:52:02,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much.

892
00:52:02,498 --> 00:52:03,839
[SPEAKER_00]: It's beyond this situation.

893
00:52:03,899 --> 00:52:04,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

894
00:52:04,659 --> 00:52:07,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Or at least, it allows possibility to be beyond this situation.

895
00:52:08,021 --> 00:52:09,782
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is rooted... Yeah.

896
00:52:10,022 --> 00:52:15,645
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is rooted in you not wanting... Well, rooted in you feeling the pressure of the situation.

897
00:52:15,765 --> 00:52:15,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

898
00:52:16,105 --> 00:52:17,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Which you crave against.

899
00:52:17,286 --> 00:52:18,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why you're wanting this sex.

900
00:52:18,986 --> 00:52:20,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why you then engage with assumptions.

901
00:52:22,068 --> 00:52:22,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

902
00:52:22,828 --> 00:52:23,849
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is beginningless.

903
00:52:25,166 --> 00:52:27,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Gratuitous, but beginningless, as the Buddha said.

904
00:52:27,307 --> 00:52:30,388
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no discernible beginning before which avijjā wasn't.

905
00:52:30,889 --> 00:52:33,090
[SPEAKER_00]: There was no ignorance, and suddenly it became ignorant.

906
00:52:33,510 --> 00:52:35,091
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why it comes hand in hand.

907
00:52:35,291 --> 00:52:39,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Not expecting something outside or beyond, and...

908
00:52:41,220 --> 00:52:42,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Resisting and accepting... No, you can expect.

909
00:52:42,501 --> 00:52:48,264
[SPEAKER_00]: You can expect things outside, and then include your expectation within the domain of thought, fully contained within it.

910
00:52:48,324 --> 00:52:48,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

911
00:52:49,084 --> 00:52:49,364
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean...

912
00:52:49,384 --> 00:52:52,586
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, literally, with this, I have these expectations.

913
00:52:52,686 --> 00:52:53,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

914
00:52:53,746 --> 00:52:58,228
[SPEAKER_00]: With manifestations of this necessary basis, these things are possible.

915
00:52:58,329 --> 00:52:58,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

916
00:52:59,089 --> 00:53:00,770
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's Parthitsa Upadhyay's definition.

917
00:53:01,270 --> 00:53:05,132
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I was just trying to say that, like, that not...

918
00:53:09,168 --> 00:53:18,974
[SPEAKER_01]: The extent to which you're expecting something external, outside, is to do with how your inability to withstand what's present.

919
00:53:19,194 --> 00:53:24,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that's more like now on the level of sensual assumptions, the extent of the desire and stuff.

920
00:53:25,057 --> 00:53:37,985
[SPEAKER_00]: But, when you have obviously kind of withdrawn yourself from that, the expectation of how external speech is, it's not necessarily corresponding to how much you don't want your thoughts or something like that.

921
00:53:39,366 --> 00:53:49,829
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically just another principle that kind of fits into your already existing assumptions.

922
00:53:50,069 --> 00:53:52,090
[SPEAKER_01]: So it gets taken by it.

923
00:53:52,110 --> 00:53:54,931
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why once you abandon the assumptions, these principles can still remain.

924
00:53:55,431 --> 00:53:56,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking, speaking...

925
00:53:57,271 --> 00:54:02,073
[SPEAKER_00]: But you won't be able to think, speak or act out of lust, out of aversion, out of those

926
00:54:04,017 --> 00:54:14,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Perversions, they're direct result of ignorance, direct result of assumptions, of chasing the pleasure, chasing the external or getting rid of the... whatever.

927
00:54:14,226 --> 00:54:19,911
[SPEAKER_00]: Assuming the external to basically overcome the unpleasantly enduring pressure of the feeling you're subjected to.

928
00:54:19,931 --> 00:54:21,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that the external is...

929
00:54:22,093 --> 00:54:27,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that all of it has to be rooted in the container of your craving against this feeling.

930
00:54:33,933 --> 00:54:42,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, when you're not speaking, when you go back to your kuti, you're not speaking, think about possibility to speak, because it has to be done presently.

931
00:54:42,757 --> 00:54:43,657
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't be careless.

932
00:54:43,837 --> 00:54:43,997
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

933
00:54:44,438 --> 00:54:52,241
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't get distracted while you're thinking about a possibility of speaking, but not speaking.

934
00:54:52,261 --> 00:54:56,843
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's already a form of mindful thinking, or less unmindful thinking.

935
00:55:03,483 --> 00:55:08,227
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what we said at the beginning, that you cannot speak and still not be in the jhāna.

936
00:55:08,247 --> 00:55:10,289
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not all you have to do.

937
00:55:10,489 --> 00:55:11,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you not in jhāna?

938
00:55:11,770 --> 00:55:20,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the domain of the... the extent of this domain of speech, which includes possibility of breaking into speech, hasn't been understood.

939
00:55:21,158 --> 00:55:23,080
[SPEAKER_00]: And it hasn't been understood because you haven't been thinking about it.

940
00:55:25,168 --> 00:55:31,251
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to think about it, one has to dwell on the present possibility to speak.

941
00:55:31,591 --> 00:55:32,031
[SPEAKER_01]: To speak.

942
00:55:32,852 --> 00:55:50,260
[SPEAKER_00]: And then start discerning the kind of gratuitous implication of outside, which is a characteristic of that possibility at the moment, and then put that characteristic within the possibility, not maintain its notion that it's separate from it and outside of it.

943
00:55:51,460 --> 00:55:51,800
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

944
00:55:51,860 --> 00:55:54,981
[SPEAKER_00]: So, put that which you're putting first, put it second.

945
00:55:55,981 --> 00:56:00,522
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the clearest description of how to enter jhāna I've ever heard.

946
00:56:01,422 --> 00:56:02,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

947
00:56:02,462 --> 00:56:05,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

948
00:56:05,023 --> 00:56:07,883
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop that, jhāna is the necessary result.

949
00:56:08,103 --> 00:56:12,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, oh, we'll do this technique and that, and then, hopefully, it will happen to you.

950
00:56:12,624 --> 00:56:12,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

951
00:56:13,364 --> 00:56:16,705
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you do all the meditation with that implicit hope.

952
00:56:17,665 --> 00:56:23,870
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's tied in with understanding, with clarity, with imperturbability.

